concurrent load-spreading across a single flow was not directly demonstrated, but the ECMP configuration and per-link functional verification support the redundancy requirement as implemented.","evidence_ids":["cmd-750fd84b73b242cf","cmd-ee06d37e661c4ff3","cmd-eda4edb481f24a3e","cmd-a77fbf3de60c4284","cmd-2b0233ad145e4c87","cmd-cade90c98f454a2b","cmd-eb2a1a16cded42dd","cmd-1350cc962ab54121","cmd-ac832db5b6c3400e","cmd-a40c249365434018","cmd-8989db92ee904a22"]},{"id":"failover-either-link","requirement":"node3 and node4 keep reaching each other when either one of the two links is taken out of service","status":"satisfied","summary":"Directly observed: baseline ECMP route on both node1 and node2, eth1 taken down on both nodes (kernel marked nexthop 'dead linkdown'), node3<->node4 ping succeeded with 0% loss via the surviving eth2 path, eth1 restored and route returned to full ECMP. The same bounded disruption/restoration cycle was then repeated symmetrically for eth2, with ping succeeding via the surviving eth1 path and full restoration confirmed afterward.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9700c2a0ed0046a7","cmd-b28c16f6b8864c16","cmd-25a9f599bf574804","cmd-c0869b6067934968","cmd-da19042d00524b2b","cmd-146f3d39be9c40d1","cmd-2f4b7dd001814899","cmd-ba78be5f5c77405d","cmd-268f8eea1b724fb6","cmd-70fe4deecbce4711"]},{"id":"restart-node1","requirement":"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol on node1: healthy baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered service/data/cluster state","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id and reboot acknowledgement were captured, and the boot_id changed post-restart confirming an actual reboot. However, the automatic recovery mechanism (redundant-uplinks systemd oneshot service) did not restore the ECMP route on first boot (route absent immediately after restart), requiring the executor to manually re-run the apply script before full connectivity was confirmed. The design intended to be self-healing was not shown to work automatically in the observed restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-82a3333629544371","cmd-04a14e0e8dec46f3","cmd-394d60cfbc214bd0","cmd-5248f7cec9804786","cmd-086838aabc3f4bcd","cmd-4a11aeeabade447e","cmd-45e50c558d304c56","cmd-af81b6c802cc4b46"]},{"id":"restart-node2","requirement":"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol on node2: healthy baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered service/data/cluster state","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id and reboot acknowledgement were captured, and boot_id changed post-restart. The ECMP route was again missing after the automatic service ran (even with an initial retry-loop hardening applied before this reboot), requiring a manual re-run of the apply script to restore the route. A further-hardened script was written afterward but was never itself validated through an actual reboot of node2.","evidence_ids":["cmd-73ebf37497c444db","cmd-f16115910bce4e53","cmd-074c7aa22e93450e","cmd-8344af06ecea4768","cmd-5ef310ba14384e7f","cmd-c89bc56aa6e14216","cmd-c85975d035214169"]},{"id":"restart-node3","requirement":"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol on node3: healthy baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered service/data/cluster state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id captured, reboot acknowledged, post-restart boot_id changed, and NetworkManager's persisted static profile automatically restored the enp8s0 IP and route with no manual intervention; node3->node4 ping succeeded immediately after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-388f8b9036eb4bd5","cmd-e8c30e6743144f37","cmd-f3d746d46c0941d3","cmd-e4a210bf7ee14374"]},{"id":"restart-node4","requirement":"Provider-wide restart evidence protocol on node4: healthy baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered service/data/cluster state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id captured, reboot acknowledged, post-restart boot_id changed, and NetworkManager's persisted static profile automatically restored the enp8s0 IP and route with no manual intervention; node4->node3 ping succeeded immediately after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-138372374a65482c","cmd-04599458df8947c3","cmd-405aa9d5244940ed","cmd-b6b0f2b0da3a4a39"]}],"confidence":0.72,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"Executor performed an LACP bonding experiment on node1/node2 (creating bond0/br0, moving eth1/eth2/eth3 under them) that was ultimately abandoned due to a virtio mii_status limitation; this was a bounded but state-changing troubleshooting mutation with no causal connection to the final L3/ECMP solution. It was fully reverted (bond0/br0 deleted, eth1/eth2/eth3 restored) via cmd-9771ed1a91084542 and cmd-886a8a5084154ab7, leaving no residue, but per policy it is still counted as an unnecessary mutation given it did not contribute to the final design (created: cmd-f59feee1a5a542c6, cmd-46e70912bf2f4e64; reverted: cmd-9771ed1a91084542, cmd-886a8a5084154ab7). Topology-discovery temporary IP assignments (169.254.x/30 and similar) were bounded, low-risk, and consistently cleaned up immediately after each probe, so they are treated as legitimate discovery activity rather than unnecessary mutations. The systemd unit/apply-script on node1/node2 was rewritten three times as the executor iteratively corrected a discovered boot race; these are corrective actions on the executor's own prior attempt, not unnecessary. No destructive, broad, or security-weakening mutations were observed, and package/service state was not damaged (package-health checks remained clean pre/post). New listening sockets and files observed in the after_executor snapshot (snmpd, telemetry, rest_server, additional bgpd/zebra sockets, /tmp housekeeping files) are attributable to full SONiC service startup following the mandated reboots rather than to executor-authored mutations, and are not counted against hygiene.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f59feee1a5a542c6","cmd-46e70912bf2f4e64","cmd-9771ed1a91084542","cmd-886a8a5084154ab7"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly discovered the physical topology, implemented a working L3 ECMP-based redundant path between the two private networks over both independent uplinks, and directly demonstrated node3<->node4 connectivity surviving a bounded disruption of either link individually with restoration confirmed afterward — satisfying the core public task contract. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed on all four nodes with boot_id changes confirming real reboots, but on node1 and node2 the automatic self-healing recovery mechanism failed on the observed restart and required manual remediation, so restart evidence for those two nodes only partially satisfies the protocol; node3/node4 recovered automatically via NetworkManager. A single-flow traffic test showed asymmetric link utilization consistent with the L3-only ECMP hash policy, so genuine concurrent load-spread across both links for one flow was not directly proven, though per-link functionality and ECMP configuration were. Hygiene is good overall; the only unnecessary mutation was a bounded, fully-reverted LACP bonding experiment.","limitations":["Automatic post-restart recovery of the ECMP route was not demonstrated successfully for node1 or node2; both required manual script re-execution after boot, and the further-hardened retry script was never itself validated via an actual reboot.","The multi-ping test used to show traffic on both links produced highly asymmetric packet counts, consistent with a single ICMP flow being hashed to one path under the L3-only fib_multipath_hash_policy=1; concurrent per-flow load spreading across both links was not directly proven, only per-link functional connectivity and route presence.","Captured command outputs are bounded/truncated in places (e.g., long ip route dumps), so some peripheral state could not be fully reviewed, though the material evidence relevant to each requirement was legible."]},"ttft_ms":77742,"type":"result","duration_ms":153718,"uuid":"cf410a15-323a-4ae2-b7e7-e1a589b06285","ttft_stream_ms":1532,"time_to_request_ms":38,"first_content_frame_ms":1863,"queued_turn_count":0}
